u=1$, we have $I_0(2) - I_2(2) = I_1(2)$, which implies $I_2(2) = I_0(2) - I_1(2)$. Substituting this into the expression for $C$, we get $C = 1 +  rac{I_0(2) - I_1(2)}{I_1(2)} = 1 +  rac{I_0(2)}{I_1(2)} - 1 =  rac{I_0(2)}{I_1(2)}$.
4. Express $C'$ in terms of Bessel functions. The modified continued fraction is $C' = 2 +  rac{1}{3 +  rac{1}{4 +  rac{1}{5 + \dots}}}$. Using the identity from Step 2 with $
u=3$, we have $ rac{I_3(2)}{I_2(2)} =  rac{1}{3 +  rac{1}{4 +  rac{1}{5 + \dots}}}$. Thus, $C' = 2 +  rac{I_3(2)}{I_2(2)}$. We want to relate this back to $I_0(2)$ and $I_1(2)$ or directly to $C$.
