6. Relate $C'$ to $C$. From Step 3, we found $C =  rac{I_0(2)}{I_1(2)}$. From Step 5, we found $C' =  rac{I_1(2)}{I_2(2)}$. We also know from the recurrence relation with $
u=1$ that $I_2(2) = I_0(2) - I_1(2)$. Substituting this into the expression for $C'$, we get $C' =  rac{I_1(2)}{I_0(2) - I_1(2)}$.
7. Express $C'$ as a function of $C$. Divide the numerator and denominator of the expression for $C'$ by $I_1(2)$. This gives $C' =  rac{I_1(2)/I_1(2)}{I_0(2)/I_1(2) - I_1(2)/I_1(2)} =  rac{1}{ rac{I_0(2)}{I_1(2)} - 1}$. Since $C =  rac{I_0(2)}{I_1(2)}$, we substitute $C$ into the equation to find $C' =  rac{1}{C - 1}$.
